---
description: Object detection and vision rules for coverage, privacy, and latency-sensitive deployments
version: 1.7.1
globs: [
  "object-detection/**",
  "vision/**",
  "detection/**",
  "ml/**",
  "ai/**",
  "models/**",
  "apps/**/vision/**",
  "packages/**/vision/**",
  "**/*.ipynb",
  "**/*.py",
  "**/*.yaml",
  "**/*.yml"
]
alwaysApply: false
---

- Choose architectures to match device budgets: optimize for real-time latency (quantization/pruning) while keeping accuracy targets explicit.
- Ensure datasets cover deployment environments: lighting, weather, camera optics (including fisheye), viewpoints, occlusion, motion blur, and small-object cases.
- Handle wide/FOV or fisheye cameras intentionally: either rectify frames with calibration or train fisheye-aware models; validate edge-of-frame performance.
- Use clear train/val/test splits and track drift; re-evaluate when environments change or new classes appear.
- Augment realistically (scales, crops, brightness/contrast/noise) but avoid augmentations that reduce label fidelity for small/critical objects.
- Apply privacy-first masking for sensitive categories (faces, plates) post-detection; never store raw payloads longer than required.
- Encrypt video/frame storage and transport; set retention ceilings and restrict access paths to least-privilege roles.
- Evaluate end-to-end: latency/throughput on target hardware, detection quality (mAP/recall), and robustness to load; keep budgets and results documented.
- Set concrete budgets per tier and enforce in CI/canary:
  - Edge/Standard: ≤120 ms/frame @ ≥25 FPS target, mAP@0.5 ≥0.50, recall ≥0.80 on key classes.
  - Enhanced: ≤80 ms/frame @ ≥30 FPS, mAP@0.5 ≥0.60, recall ≥0.85, stable under burst load (2–3× nominal).
  - Centralized: ≤50 ms/frame (or ≥40 FPS equivalent) with batching allowed, mAP@0.5 ≥0.65 and mAP@0.5:0.95 ≥0.40; regression guardrails block merges on drop >2% absolute.
- Smooth detections with stable tracking/filters to reduce jitter; pad boxes slightly to avoid under-masking small targets.
- Validate pipelines with unit tests (pre/post-processing), integration tests (detect → mask → store/search), and performance tests under load.
